== Part 4a: one response, invariance to scaling by 1e-9 / 1e9 ==
  dirs, cuts, selected.terms unchanged at 1e-9 and 1e9, in 5/5 configs

== Part 4b: three responses, does multiplying one response by 1000 change the terms? ==
  scaling one of three responses by 1000 changed dirs or selected.terms in 5/5 configs

CHECK bb17.1 TRUE one response, no weights: the first RssBeforeAddingHinge equals n-1 for n=50,200 and for a y with a large mean and scale
CHECK bb17.2 TRUE one response, weighted: RssBeforeAddingHinge equals sum(w*(y-weighted.mean(y,w))^2)/var(y), the PLAIN unweighted sd(y); none of the weighted-variance denominators (sum(w)-1, sum(w), n-1) or no-scaling match (0,0,0,0 matches of 24)
CHECK bb17.3a TRUE three responses, default: the first RssBeforeAddingHinge is the sum over responses of the unscaled TSS, in all 5 configs, even with wildly different per-response scales
CHECK bb17.3b TRUE three responses, Scale.y=TRUE: the first RssBeforeAddingHinge is K*(n-1), i.e. each response is standardized to sd 1 before summing, in all 5 configs
CHECK bb17.4a TRUE one response: scaling y by 1e-9 or 1e9 leaves dirs, cuts and selected.terms unchanged (5/5 configs)
CHECK bb17.4b TRUE three responses: multiplying one response by 1000 changes dirs or selected.terms, confirming the default criterion is the RAW summed RSS with no per-response scaling (5/5 configs)
